-- #1617/#1618 BYSTANDER — the new machinery is PRESENT and nothing asserted
-- here uses it.
--
-- "The feature works" is what the sibling fixtures test. This one tests the
-- question no gate normally asks: is the code that has NOTHING to do with the
-- change still correct? The change adds a new head-tag bucket (`__fun__`) and
-- makes `headTyNode` peel effect rows, both of which are consulted by EVERY
-- dispatch in the program, not only by the ones that need them.
--
-- 🚨 AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS FIXTURE COULD NOT FAIL, and an adversarial
-- reviewer falsified it by adding ONE method to its own interface. It declared
-- an arrow impl and an effect impl and then asserted only ORDINARY dispatch,
-- where the goal head is concrete — so the readers the change actually
-- perturbed were never consulted at all. Those readers do not decide ROUTING,
-- they decide ACCEPTANCE.
--
-- The multi-param `Conv` block below is one of them, reachable from a fixture
-- that must also BUILD: `groundOneObligation` grounds `b` by asking for the
-- UNIQUE `Conv` impl at head tag `Int`, and if the effect-headed sibling were
-- counted there would be two matches and `b` would stay undetermined — on an
-- impl that is never dispatched to.
--
-- 🚨 MEASURED ON THE COMMIT THAT HAD THE NARROWING (`a8a226cb`), THIS FILE
-- SEGFAULTS — and the shape of that failure is why this fixture is worth more
-- than a rejection would be. There: `check` exits 0, `run` prints the right
-- `(48, True, True)`, `build` exits 0, and the BUILT BINARY dies with
-- `E-FATAL-SIGNAL: fatal memory fault (segmentation fault)`, reproducibly. The
-- ungrounded obligation is also a missing dict parameter, so the front end
-- accepts a program the emitter then mis-elaborates. On base `aaa43716` and on
-- the amended branch all four cells agree on `(48, True, True)`.
--
-- ⚠️ THE SECOND ACCEPTANCE READER — the impl-head COUNT behind
-- `T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE` on an UNDETERMINED constraint — is NOT asserted here,
-- deliberately, and the reason is not that it does not matter. An undetermined
-- constraint leaves the site's route unstamped, and the emitter then E-PANICs
-- `no impl of method '…' for type '…'` on EVERY arm including the pre-change
-- base — a single-impl interface with no arrow and no effect anywhere still
-- fails to `build` on base `aaa43716` — so a fixture in a corpus that BUILDS
-- cannot carry that shape without going red for a defect it does not own. It is
-- asserted where it belongs, in a CHECK-only corpus:
-- `test/check_json_fixtures/impl_head_census_acceptance.mdk`, whose golden is
-- an EMPTY diagnostics array and which `a8a226cb` reddens.
--
-- Expected `(48, True, True)`, hand-derived slot by slot, and byte-identical to
-- what the pre-change compiler printed:
--   1. `unwrap (Box 41)` = 41; `sz True` = 7 via `impl Sz Bool` (NOT the arrow
--      impl, which `Bool` cannot match, and NOT the effect impl, whose head
--      peels to `Float`); 41 + 7 = 48.
--   2. `[1, 2, 3] == [1, 2, 3]` = `True` — prelude dispatch that predates all
--      of this.
--   3. `conv 1` grounds `b` from the UNIQUE `Conv` impl at head `Int` — the
--      effect-headed `Conv (<Stdout> Int) String` is not counted — giving
--      `impl Conv Int Bool`, so `conv 1` is `1 > 0` = `True`.
interface Sz a where
  sz : a -> Int

impl Sz Bool where
  sz _ = 7

-- present, never dispatched to
impl Sz (Int -> Int) where
  sz _ = 5

-- present, never dispatched to
impl Sz (<Stdout> Float) where
  sz _ = 6

interface Conv a b where
  conv : a -> b

impl Conv Int Bool where
  conv n = n > 0

-- present, never dispatched to
impl Conv (<Stdout> Int) String where
  conv _ = "never"

data Box a = Box a

unwrap : Box Int -> Int
unwrap (Box n) = n

main = println (unwrap (Box 41) + sz True, [1, 2, 3] == [1, 2, 3], conv 1)
